.TH RECIPE 1 "2026-11-16" "Recipe" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
recipe \- execute Rhai package recipes through Ginux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B recipe
[options] download|build|install|update|remove|cleanup|state recipe
.br
.B recipe
[options] cleanup [\-\-logs]
.br
.B recipe
[options] list|installed|updates|storage|history|index|info|hash|refresh [argument]
.br
.B recipe
\-\-system [options] install|update|remove|state recipe
.br
.B recipe
\-\-system [options] installed
.br
.B recipe
\-\-system [\-\-root DIR] apply\-release folder
.br
.B recipe
\-\-system \-\-root TREE record\-base \-\-release FILE
.br
.B recipe
runtime status|fetch|stage|remove
.br
.B recipe
[options] source list|add|remove [argument]
.br
.B recipe
[options] validate|inspect|explain|diff [arguments]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Recipe reads a recipe (an API 1 Rhai file, see recipe-recipe(5)), downloads its
sources, checks them, unpacks them, places the listed files, runs the recipe's
own build code when it has some, finishes the result, installs it, removes it
and cleans up. It does these steps itself from the recipe's declarations; a
recipe says what a program is and where it comes from, not how to fetch or
install it. Code the recipe does contain runs in a configured Ginux domain, or on
the host when the recipe starts no program, and the named outputs are published
after successful execution and independent archive validation. A recipe that can
start a program always runs in Ginux; a recipe that cannot runs as a confined
host process. See EXECUTION MODES.
.PP
Every recipe declares const RECIPE_API = 1 and a literal const NEEDS. NEEDS owns
typed input and output names, network endpoints and upper limits; callers bind
inputs and can only narrow authority. Recipe decides what is done from its own
records (a download by its checksum, a result by its identity, an installed
program by its receipt); no recipe code runs to decide it. Only values the recipe
saved with save() are restored after an interruption, together with their
checkpoint. Final JSON carries the published host paths in outputs.
.PP
OS artifact producers and user-managed software use the same format and engine.
In the LevitateOS workspace, distro/builder/os-recipes is the builder's collection
and the top-level recipes directory is the user catalog (starting with ripgrep).
The builder composes OS images from published outputs; recab owns the two roots of an A/B system.
The CLI activates directory outputs named package through an atomic profile switch.
The library and builder (--no-activate) continue to consume outputs only.
.SH STEPS AND STATES
A program has four steps. download fetches its sources and pins into the download
store. build unpacks the sources and packages, places the recipe's FILES, runs
its build or configure function, finishes a system tree, runs its verify
function and keeps the result. install activates a package output, writes a
system output into the root, or publishes a producer's outputs. cleanup deletes
the program's downloads, unpacked sources, build material and saved state.
install does every step still missing, in order, and then cleanup, unless the
keep option is on. Each step can also be run alone. A recipe without sources and
packages has no download step of its own; a recipe without a build function
shows no build step.
.PP
The state of a program is none (nothing of it here, or only part of its
downloads), downloaded (all its sources and pins for this system are in the
download store), built (a result with the current identity is kept, not
installed) or installed (the receipt lists it). The identity of a build is the
recipe's text, its base, its modules, its pins file, the checksums of the selected
sources and pins, its inputs' contents, its defines, its dependencies'
identities, the prefix, the root image and the engine's behaviour versions.
.PP
After a successful install the downloads, the unpacked source, the build material
and the saved state are deleted, unless the keep option is on; files are moved
into place, not copied, where both places are on one disk. A download two
programs name is deleted only when neither still needs it. remove frees the
program's space at once: there are no earlier versions and no go-back. Recipe
reports what each program holds on disk beside its installed files: downloads,
build material (unpacked sources, work folder, a result not installed) and an
unfinished run's saved state. These rules also apply to producer runs
(--no-activate, the OS builder), with install meaning outputs published.
.SH RUNTIME SELECTION
Installed defaults are libexec/recipe/ginux,
share/recipe/runtime/rootfs.tar with its root-reference file and
share/recipe/runtime/minimal.tar with its minimal-reference file, under the
prefix of the recipe binary (/usr for /usr/bin/recipe), else under /usr. A recipe
with NEEDS.runtime "minimal" runs in the minimal image, selected with
GINUX_MINIMAL_ROOT and GINUX_MINIMAL_ROOTFS as the full image is with GINUX_ROOT
and GINUX_ROOTFS; where none is named it runs in the full image. Explicit
environment selections override these defaults. When the image tar is neither
installed nor in $XDG_CACHE_HOME/recipe/images, Recipe downloads it on first use
and checks its SHA-256. RECIPE_RUNTIME_URL names another address; {digest} and
{short} (the first 16 digits) are replaced.
Set GINUX_BIN to the actual launcher executable, GINUX_ROOTFS to an assembled
rootfs tar and GINUX_ROOT to oci:name@sha256:digest matching that tar. Set
RECIPE_WORKER_BIN when the worker cannot be resolved from the current CLI/PATH.
The launcher must support configured downstream catalog 1002 and configured-network-audit=1
and configured-denial-audit=1.
The current native launcher needs Linux x86_64 and chroot bootstrap privileges.
Run the whole coordinator with those privileges and the explicit launcher,
image and matching worker selections. Wrapping only the launcher in sudo loses
required inherited channels. Select the compatible maintained source pair in
docs/setup-linux.md. Later local consumer corrections require their recorded
final source pair; the bootstrap baseline does not certify that final release.
Build the image explicitly with recipe-image; ordinary execution consumes it.
See bootstrap/README.md for the pinned manifest producer.
.SH EXECUTION MODES
Before a run, Recipe compiles (never runs) each recipe of the graph, the base
it extends and every module it imports. A recipe that can reach exec,
exec_checked, exec_output, shell*, git_checkout, target_extract_tar,
command_exists, rpm_installed, rpm_version, dnf_*, apk_*, carry_link_flags or
carry_libraries, or that uses eval, Fn with a computed name or a dynamic import,
runs in Ginux. Every other recipe runs on the host as an ordinary process of the
invoking user (run by root: under a uid and gid of its own from 1879048192 up,
without groups or capabilities, in /var/lib/recipe/host-runs): no launcher, image or namespace is
needed. There Recipe's helpers resolve every path beneath the run's inputs
(read-only), work and out and refuse paths using .., the HTTP client reaches
only NEEDS.network at pinned addresses (a host name resolving to a loopback or
private address is refused) within network_max_bytes, the program helpers are
not registered, and a seccomp filter refuses execve and new processes.
The recipe sees host paths for its inputs, work folder and output. Checkpoints, restore, publication and
the deadline work as in Ginux; storage bounds are checked at checkpoints and at
the end. Each run states which way every recipe went and why.
.SH SYSTEM SCOPE
Software for the computer's root, not for one user: \fB\-\-system\fR with
install, update, remove, installed or state. Without --package-root, root
running install, update, remove, installed or state on an A/B system takes
the same route.
.PP
An A/B system is one whose kernel command line has recab.immutable. Its booted
root is locked and its other root is unlocked. Recipe asks \fBrecab status
\-\-json\fR where the other root is mounted (other_mount; RECIPE_RECAB_BIN names
another recab) and writes the change there, never into the booted root. The
change becomes active when the other root is booted (recab boot-other, then a
restart). If the other root is not mounted, Recipe says so and stops; recab copy
recreates it. Packages go under the other root's usr/lib/recipe/profile, recorded
in its usr/lib/recipe/system.json.
.PP
Recipe records what it wrote in other-root.json in its package root
(/var/lib/recipe/other-root.json for root). recab copy makes the other root a
copy of the booted root and so wipes those changes. Before any system-scope
command, Recipe compares the record with both roots' receipts: a change the
booted root now holds was booted into and is forgotten; a change neither root
holds was wiped, and Recipe writes exactly that system part again, not the whole
install and never software installed for one user, and then runs the root's
effects and library cache once, as after any system change (a failure is a warning
and the parts stay written). Until that succeeds the part is reported as not done.
.PP
With --json, --system installed prints the booted root's packages and the other
root's, and the changes not done. On a system without recab.immutable there is one
root, the running one, and no recab: --system writes the change into it, with its
usr/lib/recipe/system.json, exactly as into any root (RECIPE_BOOTED_ROOT names
another directory for tests). --system --root DIR does the same for DIR, a root that
is not running: an installer's /mnt or an image tree. Neither calls recab or keeps
other-root.json; --root refuses the booted root of an A/B system.
--package-root DIR and --no-activate keep their own routes. Without --system, root's
install on a system without recab.immutable still uses /var/lib/recipe.
.PP
Writing into a root: a system output's owner names are resolved against that root's
etc/passwd and etc/group. Accounts the change needs are created first, from its own
usr/lib/sysusers.d/*.conf files and the accounts its recipe declares, the way the OS
builder creates them (g lines, then u lines; existing accounts kept; "-" is the
highest free id below 1000), and recorded with the package in the receipt; removing
the package keeps them. Account lines may also be m USER GROUP (group membership).
Recipe finishes every system tree so that its var/ holds state seeds, created in the
system's /var only where missing (on an A/B system the running root's shared /var),
never overwritten, owned or removed. A configuration file under etc/ that was changed since it was
installed is kept and reported: a removal leaves it, an update writes the new version
beside it as NAME.recipe-new. Any other changed file refuses the change. Directories
left empty by a removal are removed; directories the root had before are never
recorded. One syncfs flushes each change before the receipt is written. The
producer's published outputs are deleted once written (its saved state stays unless
--no-persist-state; --state-dir and --build-dir choose where both live).
.PP
\fB\-\-system apply\-release FOLDER\fR applies a fetched release (release.json,
base.tar.zst, recipes.tar.zst) to the root above. It refuses another os than the
root's os-release ID and a serial lower than the root's
/usr/lib/levitate/release.json, and checks the archives' SHA-256 first. The base
tree replaces the reserved receipt entry base (edited etc/ files kept with
NAME.recipe-new beside them; fstab, crypttab, hostname, machine-id, localtime,
adjtime, mtab, resolv.conf, subuid, subgid, ld.so.cache, the account files and
\&.recipe\-target\-metadata.json never part of it;
missing release accounts added), the manifest becomes
usr/lib/levitate/release.json, every held package the release has NAME.rhai for
is installed from it, and the effects and the library cache run once. The groups are installed from the
new base's usr/lib/recipe/release-recipes (written by then), which later commands
use as the release source; only a base without that folder has recipes.tar.zst
unpacked under /var/lib/recipe/release/SERIAL for the apply, removed at its end. On the running root the running
kernel's old modules stay as the hidden package previous-kernel. A root without
base is refused. Without A/B, /var/lib/recipe/apply-release.json records the step,
and the same command finishes an interrupted apply. \fBrecord\-base\fR writes base
for an image tree at build time. base is listed by installed \-\-json only, and
cannot be installed, updated or removed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B download RECIPE
The download step only: fetch the recipe's sources and the pins for this system
into the download store (the offline cache is used first). Nothing is unpacked.
.TP
.B build RECIPE
Download what is missing, then build: unpack the sources and packages, place
FILES, run build or configure, finish a system tree and run verify. The result is
kept, not installed. The materials stay until install or cleanup.
.TP
.B install RECIPE
Run every missing step in order (download, build, install), then cleanup unless
the keep option is on, and publish the named products.
A directory output named package activates regular bin/COMMAND and share/PATH
files in the selected package root's profile, unless --no-activate is set.
Before anything is downloaded, Recipe compares the C library the recipe declares
(ABOUT.libc) with this system's, for the recipe and its runtime dependencies.
When they differ, Recipe prints what the build is linked against, what this
system's C library is, and the command that installs it anyway
(\fB\-\-any\-libc\fR), and exits with a nonzero status; with \fB\-\-json\fR it
also prints a record whose kind is libc_mismatch.
With \fB\-\-system\fR, see SYSTEM SCOPE.
.TP
.B update RECIPE
Install the version the recipe as fetched names, replacing the installed one.
Never edits a recipe and never installs a version the recipe does not name. A
failure preserves the old profile; the replaced files are deleted once the new
ones are active.
.TP
.B remove RECIPE [\-\-keep]
Remove the installed files by the receipt, without a recipe or Ginux, and then
whatever is left of the program's downloads and build material; its space is
freed at once. With \fB\-\-keep\fR only the installed program is removed and its
downloads and build material stay. Remove forgets the program's keep option.
With --no-activate (or an output-only producer) the published outputs and the
program's material are removed instead; prior publications stay immutable.
.TP
.B installed
List active packages and versions from the installed receipt, without Ginux.
With --json each package also names what its recipe was allowed to use when it
was installed (needs), when (installed_on) and the length of its files
(size_bytes).
.TP
.B updates [\-\-upstream] [NAME...]
Say which installed packages update would change, and what the newer recipe may
use that the installed one may not (the NEEDS recorded at install, compared).
Reads the receipts, the collection and the sources as last fetched; fetches and
runs nothing. With \fB\-\-upstream\fR (optionally with recipe names as plain
arguments) Recipe also asks upstream for each installed program, or each named
recipe, through its UPSTREAM declaration or its latest() function, and tells what
it found in a separate list (installed, recipe_version, latest, newer, how,
error). It only tells: a recipe is never edited and a version the recipe does not
name is never installed. A package has an update when its recipe names another version or
its source publishes another recipe file for it. A package whose name the release
source carries (see \fBsource\fR) is at the release's version and is never listed.
.TP
.B history
List what was done to the installed software, newest first: installs, updates
and removals. Reads
history.jsonl in the package root, without Ginux; a line that cannot be read is
left out. Each entry has at (seconds since 1970), what, package, from, to and
source, null where one does not apply. The log keeps the newest entries. With
--json (and when piped) prints {"schema_version": 1, "history": [...]}.
.TP
.B storage
Show everything Recipe holds on disk, by kind, each byte counted once. With
--json, always these seven kinds in this order, each with its bytes, whether it
can be removed, the command that removes it and the programs holding it:
installed (recipe remove NAME), downloaded (recipe cleanup NAME --downloads),
built (recipe cleanup NAME --build), kept (recipe cleanup NAME; material of
installed programs with the keep option), unfinished (recipe cleanup NAME
--build; saved state of interrupted runs), build_image (the cached compiler
image, about 1.3 GB; recipe runtime remove) and logs (recipe cleanup --logs).
.TP
.B cleanup RECIPE [\-\-downloads|\-\-build]
Delete the program's downloads, unpacked sources, build material and saved state;
the installed program stays. \fB\-\-downloads\fR deletes its downloads only,
\fB\-\-build\fR its build material only (an unfinished run included). It also
deletes kept material but leaves the keep option on, so the next install keeps
again. When the recipe defines cleanup(reason), it is called with the reason
manual after the engine's own deletion. This is also how a step is redone: clean
it up and run it again; there are no force options.
.TP
.B cleanup [\-\-logs]
Without a recipe, delete the downloads and build material of every program.
\fB\-\-logs\fR deletes the diagnostic logs.
.TP
.B state RECIPE
Print the program's state (none, downloaded, built or installed) from Recipe's
own records; no recipe code runs. It exits 0 whatever the state. With --json:
name, version, state, installed_version, keep, the steps the recipe has and
on_disk (downloads, build, unfinished bytes).
.TP
.B list
List declared recipe metadata without evaluating setup/helper code.
This lists available recipe files, not installed applications.
With \fB\-\-json\fR, print one entry per recipe for a front end: name, version,
description, path, ABOUT (including its libc list), NEEDS, dependency names and the steps it has, the functions it defines, its upstream and its state on this system (none, downloaded, built or installed) with the keep option,
and for each recipe runs_here and not_runnable_reason (whether this system's C
library can run it, and the message install would give, else null), and its
source. The release source is listed first. On a root with a release source, a
recipe of a remote source that the release carries by name, or that is a system
group, has runs_here false, not_runnable_override null and a one-sentence
not_runnable_reason (\-\-any\-system does not change this). The top level carries
host_libc, this system's C library: glibc, musl or unknown. With \fB\-\-system\fR
[\fB\-\-root DIR\fR], the release source is that of the root a system change
targets (the default without \-\-system too).
.TP
.B index [FOLDER] [\-\-check]
Write index.json in a folder of recipes (default: the current directory): the
folder's repo.toml, every recipe as \fBlist \-\-json\fR prints it, the SHA-256
and size of each recipe file and of the files beside it that share its name,
and the commit they are at. The folder must be in a Git repository with full
history, and the listed files must be committed. With \fB\-\-check\fR, write
nothing and fail when index.json is missing or out of date.
.TP
.B source list
.TQ
.B source add ADDRESS [\-\-branch BRANCH]
.TQ
.B source add \-\-path FOLDER [\-\-name NAME]
.TQ
.B source remove NAME
Show, add or remove the sources recipes come from beyond the recipe folders.
A remote source is a repository on github.com or gitlab.com, or the address
its files are fetched from with {rev} and {path} in it; only its published
index is kept. A folder is read as it is. A source is one file,
sources/NAME.toml under the package root or in /usr/share/recipe/sources; a
shipped source is switched off, not removed. \fBSOURCE:NAME\fR names one
source's recipe wherever a recipe name is accepted. Naming a recipe of a
remote source downloads it with the recipes it needs, each file checked
against the index; \fBinstall\fR and \fBupdate\fR keep the copy under the
package root and record source, commit and SHA-256 in the receipt. With
\fBinstall \-\-sha256 HASH\fR, nothing runs unless the recipe file has that
checksum. An explicit \fB\-\-recipes\-path\fR uses no sources.
.IP
The release source, named \fBsystem\fR, is built in: the group recipes of the
system release run by the root a system change targets (the other root on A/B,
/ without A/B, DIR with \-\-root), which that root carries as base files in
usr/lib/recipe/release-recipes. It is read from disk, needs no index or refresh,
cannot be removed, and is absent where the root has no such folder (another
distribution, or an explicit \-\-recipes\-path). \fBsource list\fR and
\fBlist \-\-json\fR show it with kind release, the title "OS-NAME VERSION" (os-release
NAME, version from usr/lib/levitate/release.json), path, os and version. A system
group is a recipe of ABOUT.kind system, or library with a system output; groups
change only with a system release. Two rules follow on such a root. A system
change (\-\-system install, update, remove) resolves a name the release source
carries to its recipe, whatever other source carries the name (SOURCE:NAME of a
remote source included; a note says so); its dependencies resolve beside it,
within the release, and a remote recipe's dependencies the release carries come
from the release. A system group of a remote source is not installed, whether or
not the release carries its name: a newer system release brings newer groups,
and a folder of one's own (\fBsource add \-\-path\fR, or the recipe folder)
is not restricted. \fBupdates\fR and \-\-system update never take a newer
version of a name the release carries from elsewhere; a package installed from
a release's recipes that the current release no longer carries is reported
(update JSON not_in_release true, changed false; installed \-\-json
from_release and not_in_release) and not updated. A package installed from
another copy of a recipe the release carries with the same content (the install
medium's) counts as installed from the release. A recipe given as a file path
is used as it is.
.TP
.B refresh [NAME]
Fetch the index of every remote source again, or of one, over HTTPS. A failed
refresh leaves the index kept from before in use.
.TP
.B info RECIPE
Show loader-owned declared/inherited metadata. Dynamic metadata is unavailable.
A recipe with ABOUT.libc also gets a C library line: what its build is linked
against and what this system's C library is.
.TP
.B validate RECIPE [--input NAME=PATH]
Check literal declarations, typed bindings, dependencies and compiled contracts
without executing setup or launching a workload.
.TP
.B inspect RECIPE
Show declared/effective authority, dependency contracts and catalog digests.
.TP
.B explain INVOCATION|RECIPE OPERATION
Explain a static rule or retained invocation audit using its saved authority.
Absent/truncated observations remain unknown. INVOCATION may be its log directory.
.TP
.B diff OLD NEW
Compare typed inputs/outputs, endpoints, raised/lowered limits and digests.
.TP
.B hash FILE
Compute SHA256, SHA512 and BLAKE3 for a local file.
.TP
.B runtime status | fetch | stage | remove
status shows the selected runtime image, the launcher and the shipped sources
(JSON when piped or with --json). fetch downloads the image now. stage writes a
runtime tree into --root (--launcher, --reference, --catalog, optional --image
and --prefix); packagers use it. --apparmor also writes etc/apparmor.d/recipe-ginux,
which hosts that restrict unprivileged user namespaces (Ubuntu 23.10 and later)
need before an ordinary user can run recipes; load it with apparmor_parser -r.
remove deletes the cached build image (about 1.3 GB); it is fetched again when a
recipe needs it. An image installed with the system is not removed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -r, --recipes-path DIR
Directory for name, module and dependency lookup. Defaults to RECIPE_PATH or
the per-user application-data recipe directory, followed by the sources (see
\fBsource\fR), of which an image ships its own in /usr/share/recipe/sources.
Explicit --recipes-path or RECIPE_PATH uses that directory alone.
.TP
.B --system
System scope (see SYSTEM SCOPE): install, update, remove, installed and state
act on the computer's root. install takes several recipes (recipe --system install A B C) and
skips a recipe or dependency the root holds at the same recipe digest and version
(the digest names the recipe's content, not the folder it lies in). On an A/B system the change is written into the other
(unlocked) root and becomes active when that root is booted; the booted root is
never written.
.TP
.B --root DIR
With --system, given before the command: write the change into DIR, a root that is
not running, and read installed and state from DIR. No recab call, no A/B
record (see SYSTEM SCOPE).
.TP
.B --no-effects
With --system: do not run the root's effects recipe (usr/lib/recipe/effects.rhai)
after the change. Without it, a successful change runs that recipe with the root as
its read-only input root and writes its system output into the root as the reserved
package system-effects; a failure is reported and leaves the change written.
The root's glibc library cache, etc/ld.so.cache, is rebuilt after every change
with or without this option: Recipe runs the root's own ldconfig as
ldconfig \-X \-i \-r ROOT, only for a root with usr/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 and
etc/ld.so.conf (never on a musl root); a failure is reported the same way
(effects.library_cache) and leaves the change written.
.TP
.B --package-root DIR
Package generations, installed receipts and active profile symlink. Defaults to
/var/lib/recipe for root or XDG_DATA_HOME/recipe for an ordinary user.
Add DIR/profile/bin to PATH. Without an immutable system the login profile puts
/var/lib/recipe/profile/bin on every user's PATH, after the user's own store.
Only the active generation is kept.
.TP
.B --no-activate
Run the producer lifecycle only. Do not activate package outputs or remove active packages.
.TP
.B -b, --build-dir DIR
Logical destination identity and shared lock. Domain work stays /work/build.
The default destination is stable under the selected state root.
.TP
.B --state-dir DIR
State and diagnostic root. Defaults to XDG_STATE_HOME/recipe.
.TP
.B --input NAME=PATH
Bind a declared typed input read-only at /inputs/NAME. Required missing,
undeclared, duplicate and wrong-kind bindings fail before setup. Use DEP::NAME
for a dependency input; inputs are never implicitly inherited. A named target input seeds owned
/out on a fresh identity; retained state restores that target instead.
.TP
.B --effects-root PATH
Give the recipe the system root PATH as its read-only input root, live: as root
with CAP_SYS_ADMIN, in a Ginux run, outside a chroot, each top-level folder of
PATH (but var, boot, home, root, tmp, run, proc, sys, dev, mnt, media, srv,
lost+found) is bound read-only at /inputs/root in the launcher's own mount
namespace for the run, instead of hashed and copied; top-level links are kept,
top-level files left out, and the run keeps no state. Otherwise PATH is copied
like --input root=PATH. --system passes it to the root's effects recipe.
.TP
.B -d, --define KEY=VALUE
Recipe-specific scope constant. A host path in a define grants no input access.
.TP
.B --target-os linux --target-arch x86_64
Currently supported native execution target.
.TP
.B -j, --jobs N
Requested job count, 1 through 16, default 1. Ginux task limits still apply.
.TP
.B --any-libc
For install and update: go ahead when the recipe's declared C library (ABOUT.libc)
differs from this system's. One note line names the recipe, its C library and
this system's. Recipe learns this system's C library from its dynamic loaders
(glibc: the architecture's ld-linux loader, e.g. /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2; musl:
/lib/ld-musl-ARCH.so.1); with both or neither present it is unknown and
nothing is compared. The environment variable RECIPE_HOST_LIBC_ROOT names a
folder to look in instead of /; a system change sets it to the root it writes into.
.TP
.B --any-system
For install and update: go ahead when the recipe's ABOUT.systems does not list the
os-release ID of this system (or of the root a system change writes into).
.TP
.B --keep
For build, install and update: the downloads and build material stay after a
successful install. Off by default. It is remembered per program: later
installs and updates of that program keep too. The builder passes it where it
wants material kept.
.TP
.B --no-keep
For build, install and update: turn the keep option off and, when the program is
installed, delete the material at once. remove forgets the option; cleanup
deletes kept material but leaves the setting on.
.TP
.B --timeout-secs N
Lower NEEDS.timeout_secs for recipe execution, including silent commands.
The declaration defaults to 3600, range 1 through 86400.
.TP
.B --memory-mib N
Lower NEEDS.memory_mib (default declaration 2048 MiB). Ginux bounds each
process virtual address space, not aggregate tree RSS.
.TP
.B --storage-max-objects N
Lower NEEDS.storage_max_objects (default declaration 16384).
.TP
.B --storage-max-bytes N
Lower the declared writable data cap within effective memory. Hardlink data counts once.
.TP
.B --network-host HOST:PORT
Select a subset of NEEDS.network; repeatable, at most eight. An absent network
declaration grants no outbound TCP. --no-network selects an empty subset. Numeric endpoints are pinned before launch; redirects also need grants.
.TP
.B --network-max-bytes N
Lower NEEDS.network_max_bytes (default declaration 8 GiB).
.PP
Explicit limit and network restrictions apply across the dependency graph.
Each dependency keeps the stricter limit and intersects its own endpoints with
the caller subset. No restriction lends another recipe an endpoint.
.TP
.B --no-persist-state
Turn checkpoints off (see save and checkpoint in recipe-helpers(7)). Managed execution and output publication still run.
.TP
.B --execution auto|ginux|host
Where recipes run. auto (the default) runs a recipe on the host when it starts
no program and in Ginux otherwise. ginux keeps every recipe in Ginux. host
requires the host and refuses a recipe that is not eligible, naming the call
that keeps it in Ginux. See EXECUTION MODES.
.TP
.B --exact-identity
Name the worker's and the Ginux launcher's bytes in the saved-state identity, on
top of their declared behaviour versions, so only byte-identical tools reuse
saved state. Release builds use it. RECIPE_EXACT_IDENTITY=1 selects it too
(0 or empty does not; any other value is an error). Without it, Recipe and Ginux
enter the identity by ENGINE_BEHAVIOUR, GINUX_BEHAVIOUR, the transport version
and the pinned Ginux catalog and build-base digests (src/identity.rs, which
states when each is bumped); inputs, recipe closure, defines, options and the
root image are always named by content.
.TP
.B --json-output FILE
Write the final result JSON (see FINISHED RUN) to this file. Diagnostics go to stderr.
.TP
.B --progress-file PATH
Keep PATH current with the running recipe's progress() reports: one JSON object,
replaced atomically (temporary file in the same directory, then rename) at most
once a second, and at once on the first report, on a change of phase and when the
invocation ends. Fields: version (1), invocation, recipe, phase (acquire, build,
install, ...), what, done, total (0 when unknown), started and updated (RFC 3339,
UTC) and state (running, finished or failed). It is written once at the start
with done 0, total 0 and an empty what. With --machine-events a progress event
(recipe, phase, what, done, total) is also emitted.
.TP
.B --machine-events
Emit machine-readable events alongside stderr progress, one JSON object per
line with an "event" field. plan: every recipe of the run in order, with its
steps (download, build, install, cleanup). step: one step of a recipe (recipe,
step, status, msg; status is running, done, already, none, skipped or failed;
the step is download, build, install or cleanup). fetch: how far a download is (recipe, source, bytes, total;
total is null when the server did not say). progress: the recipe's progress()
reports (recipe, phase, what, done, total). The last event of a run is done
(recipe), or failed (recipe, step, reason; step is null when no step failed). A failed install
whose Ginux log records a denied connection also has refused: endpoint, the
numeric address the audit line names, and declared, the recipe's NEEDS.network.
Build progress inside a step is not reported. There is no agent event.
.SH FINISHED RUN
download, build, install, update, remove and cleanup print one result at the end
(when stdout is a terminal, a readable summary; with --json or when piped, JSON;
also written to --json-output FILE). schema_version 1. Fields: operation, name,
version, state (after the run), steps (per step of this run: step and status,
done, already when it was done before, or none when the recipe has no such step),
outputs (output name to absolute published path for a run that built or
published; empty otherwise), activation (profile and bin when a package was
activated, else null), kept, freed_bytes (deleted by cleanup or remove in this
run), dependencies (per dependency that ran: name, state, steps), contract
(the compiled contract digest) and network_usage (the bounded network summary).
A failed run exits nonzero and prints {"failure": {...}} (libc_mismatch,
system_mismatch, a denied connection's refused). A system change (--system) keeps
its own result fields (action, package, other_root, effects, ...) and adds state
and freed_bytes. installed --json carries, for each package, keep, on_disk
(downloads, build, unfinished) and update (null, or to, reason and source, known
offline from the recipes as last fetched).
.SH RECOVERY AND DIAGNOSTICS
A recipe's checkpoint (see checkpoint in recipe-helpers(7)) is kept only when its
files and its saved values are durable together; interrupted unacknowledged work
is discarded and previous publications remain. Recipe keeps one checkpoint at
most per program and deletes it when the install succeeds. An interrupted build
resumes from it.
Corrupt current state fails before startup. Preserve it for diagnosis and choose
an explicit new state directory to start fresh.
.PP
Published products are not saved state. Published paths retain coordinator ownership and
private export permissions. Privileged producers need appropriately privileged
consumers or explicit product installation; a selection symlink grants no access. Diagnostic files live in
state-root/logs/invocation-id: worker.log, supervisor-stderr.log, authority.json
and result.json. The receipt preserves the actual declaration, config, pinned
endpoints and compiled contract digest; the result includes contract and bounded network_usage, and unused-grant warnings when observation is complete.
Up to 32 recent completed diagnostic directories remain; active leases protect
in-flight logs/scratch. Later invocations reclaim recognized abandoned scratch.
Scratch is a private directory on disk, ~/.cache/recipe/scratch; RECIPE_SCRATCH_DIR
(absolute path) replaces it; a set TMPDIR selects TMPDIR/recipe-scratch-UID. Published outputs
go to STATE/exports, not the current directory.
.PP
The offline cache is a read-only directory of pinned payloads, CACHE/sha256/HEX, each
named by the SHA-256 of its bytes: RECIPE_CACHE_DIR (an absolute path; empty turns it
off), else /usr/share/recipe/cache when it exists. download and download_exact take a
payload whose pin they find there from it, with no network; a cached file whose bytes
do not match its name is ignored. A host run reads it in place; a Ginux run sees its
sha256 folder bound read-only (that needs root with CAP_SYS_ADMIN; otherwise the run
goes without it). Both run as an unprivileged identity, so only files everyone may
read are used. The cache is not an input and changes no saved-state identity.
With a cache present, a NEEDS.network host that cannot be resolved is left out with a
warning instead of failing the run, so an offline install needs no --no-network.
.PP
SIGINT/SIGTERM cancel execution and await descendant teardown without publishing
failed work. RECIPE_FRAME_TIMEOUT_SECS bounds partial-frame transport waits,
1 through 86400 seconds, default 300, independently of execution silence. A
checkpoint snapshot wait is extended while the launcher keeps writing it, up to
the execution deadline.
.PP
Where the Ginux launcher accepts it, each invocation's config enables
[storage] archive_import, so target_unpack_tree hands a whole tree to Ginux
as one stream; the launcher is asked once per launcher digest with
instance-validate. RECIPE_ARCHIVE_IMPORT=0 leaves it off (1 or empty keeps the
default; any other value is an error). Outputs are the same either way.
.PP
Where the launcher accepts the assignment names, each domain's [devices]
assignments also hold random and urandom, giving the workload /dev/random and
/dev/urandom next to /dev/null; probed once per launcher digest like archive
import. RECIPE_RANDOM_DEVICES=0 leaves them off (1 or empty keeps the default;
any other value is an error).
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
recipe install ./tool.rhai --input source=./offline-source \
  --build-dir .artifacts/work/tool --json-output .artifacts/tool.json
recipe state ./tool.rhai --input source=./offline-source \
  --build-dir .artifacts/work/tool
recipe cleanup ./tool.rhai --input source=./offline-source \
  --build-dir .artifacts/work/tool
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
Prepared tools, image libraries and required runtime operations must be present.
Network grants do not isolate virtual hosts sharing an allowed IP and port.
A recipe that starts no program runs on the host, confined as described in
EXECUTION MODES; there is no network fallback outside the declared endpoints.
Package activation copies verified publications into an owned profile; the two
roots of an A/B system belong to recab.
Raw clocks expose host realtime/monotonic time and vDSO clocks remain observable.
Configured sysinfo returns zeros, not resource accounting. CPU selection exposes
real eligible IDs, not virtual topology. Memory limits bound per-process address
space rather than aggregate process-tree RAM. Bounded poll/ppoll does not promise
general signal interruption/restart semantics. Archive timestamps have whole-second
precision. See docs/runtime-limits.md for supported variants and errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
recipe-recipe(5), recipe-helpers(7), README.md, WRITING_RECIPES.md
